<!-- 01 WHY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · Why it struggles</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">An ice machine on a truck gets the opposite of everything it was built for.</h2>

    <p>An undercounter ice machine is designed to sit in a still, ventilated back bar with steady water pressure and steady power. A truck gives it heat, a 12-volt pump, a generator and a floor that's rarely level. Our techs check those four things before they open the refrigeration side, because on a truck they're behind most low-ice calls. It's part of our <a href="/commercial/food-truck-equipment-repair/">food truck equipment repair</a> work.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">Heat</h3>
    <p>Ice production ratings are measured at mild air and water temperatures. Inside a closed truck on a summer afternoon, an air-cooled machine pulls hot air across its condenser and freezes warmer water, so each cycle runs longer and the bin fills slower. Put the fryer a few feet away and the condenser collects grease on top of dust, which cuts output again and can push the machine into a fault. On a truck we clean condensers every two to three months, not once a year.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">Water</h3>
    <p>Truck water comes from an onboard fresh water tank and a pump, not a city main. When the pump is weak, the tank is low or air gets into the line, the machine doesn't fill the way it expects in the time it expects. You get thin or partial cubes, long cycles, sometimes a water fault on the display. A clogged filter makes it worse, and hard water from the commissary fill scales the evaporator over time.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">Power</h3>
    <p>An ice machine starts its compressor over and over through the day, and every start pulls several times running current. On a loaded generator, that sag can make the machine stumble through harvest or trip off entirely. We measure voltage at the receptacle during a start, the same way we do on truck refrigeration.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">Level and vibration</h3>
    <p>Cube machines need to sit level so water runs evenly across the evaporator. A truck parked on a crowned street tilts the machine, and cubes form thick on one side and thin or not at all on the other. Driving also shakes water distribution parts, curtains and bin sensors out of place, and pops drain lines loose.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 DIAGNOSIS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · How we diagnose it</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Cheapest causes first, sealed system last.</h2>

    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Water in:</strong> pump output and pressure during fill, tank level, filter condition, air in the line.</li>
      <li><strong>Air and heat:</strong> condenser condition, clearance around the air intake, temperature where the machine breathes.</li>
      <li><strong>Power:</strong> voltage at the receptacle during compressor start and harvest.</li>
      <li><strong>Level and mechanics:</strong> the machine's level as the truck is parked, water distribution, curtain, bin control, drain.</li>
      <li><strong>Scale and cleanliness:</strong> evaporator and water system condition.</li>
      <li><strong>Refrigeration last:</strong> if all of the above check out, we test the refrigeration side. Opening a sealed system takes EPA 608 certification, and ours is Universal #1346255700410. If vibration has caused a leak, we find it and repair it rather than topping off.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>When we finish, we don't judge the machine by its first harvest. We let it run a full cycle or two, check that the cubes come out complete and even across the evaporator with the truck parked the way it usually parks, and confirm the bin control shuts production off when the bin is full. If water supply was the problem, we tell you in plain terms what the machine needs from the pump, so an upgrade gets done right the first time. Anything we cleaned or sanitized gets flushed per the manufacturer before the ice goes back into drinks.</p>

    <p>If your undercounter fridge is also warming up at events, the heat and power story is usually the same; see <a href="/commercial/food-truck-equipment-repair/refrigeration-not-cooling/">food truck fridge not cooling</a>. For brand-specific error codes and troubleshooting on Hoshizaki, Manitowoc and Scotsman machines, see our <a href="/commercial/ice-machines/">commercial ice machine repair</a> section.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CLEANING -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Clean ice</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Cleaning is part of the repair, not an extra.</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>Ice is food. A machine with scale, slime or grease inside is a health problem before it's a production problem, and an inspector will treat it that way.</p>
    </div>

    <p>We clean and sanitize using the manufacturer's procedure and approved cleaners, and we descale the evaporator and water system when scale is building. Scale insulates the evaporator, so a descaled machine often gets a good share of its lost production back with no parts at all. On a truck, heat, grease and hard water mean cleaning comes around more often than the manual's restaurant schedule suggests.</p>

    <p>What you can check before calling: the pump runs and the fresh water tank has water, the filter isn't overdue, nothing is stacked against the air intake, and the truck is parked as level as the spot allows. Leave the panels and the refrigeration side to us.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 PRICING -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">What it costs.</h2>
    <p>The diagnostic is $120, and it's waived when you approve the repair. Cleaning and descaling depend on the machine's size and condition, and parts depend on the model, so we quote both in writing after we've looked at the machine. What we quote is what you pay. When the fix turns out to be the water pump, the filter or leveling the unit, we tell you that plainly. Repairs carry our 90-day labor warranty.</p>
  </div>
</section>

